I cleaned the club signup dataset by first normalizing inconsistent values in the faculty, city, and club columns. 
I removed extra spaces, converted values to lowercase, and mapped variants such as “Media Engineering and Technology” to MET,
 “Alex” and “el giza” to their canonical cities, and “Soccer,” “Chess Club,” and “Debating” to the required club names. 
 I also standardized names by removing extra spaces and using consistent capitalization, while trimming and lowercasing emails. 
 The fee_paid column was converted from values such as yes, Y, TRUE, 1, no, N, false, and 0 into Boolean values. 
 I then parsed the two signed_up_at formats into one datetime column. The dataset started with 39 rows. 
 I first removed 3 exact duplicate rows, leaving 36 rows, then removed 4 older repeated student-club signups, leaving 32 rows. 
 I kept the latest submission because it represents the student's most recent choice. 
 I fixed club spellings before checking duplicates because this affected the results: checking before normalization found 10 rows
involved in repeated student-club combinations, compared with 8 after normalization. 
Finally, students with the same name were not merged because duplicates were identified using student_id and club, 
rather than full_name.